## Pillar 7 — The Intelligence-Native Product Canvas
*"The PRD is no longer a document. It's a living contract between Product and Engineering — grounded in organizational truth."*

Today, the most consequential decisions in a product organization happen **entirely outside the engineering intelligence system**. A PM opens a Google Doc, writes a PRD in a vacuum, passes it to engineering, and weeks of painful back-and-forth begin — because the PM didn't know the services involved have a bus factor of 1, or that a similar feature caused three production incidents in 2023, or that the acceptance criteria are technically ambiguous against the existing data model. All that organizational context exists in Nudgeio's knowledge graph. But nobody was using it at the moment the PRD was being written.

Nudgeio's Product Canvas is the **first PM-facing product built directly on an engineering knowledge graph**. It doesn't just track features after they're committed — it injects organizational intelligence into the *decision-making moment*, closes the full loop from requirement to incident, and eliminates the context collapse that happens every time a spec crosses the Product–Engineering boundary.

**The full closed loop Nudgeio now owns:**

```
PRD authored in Nudgeio (with graph intelligence)
    ↓
Graph-validated tickets with auto-assigned owners
    ↓
Commits → PRs → Code Review (tracked)
    ↓
Deployments → Production (tracked)
    ↓
Incidents → attributed back to originating requirement
    ↓
Outcome loop → feeds next PRD as institutional memory
```

### Phase 1 — Context-Grounded PRD Creation

| # | Feature | Why It's Different / Unsolved |
|---|---------|-------------------------------|
| 7.1 | **Knowledge-Grounded PRD Editor** — A collaborative document editor embedded inside Nudgeio. As a PM types a feature description, the knowledge graph surfaces in real-time: which services will be affected, who the domain experts are, what the historical incident rate is on those services, what similar features cost to build, and which past architectural decisions constrain the implementation. The PM sees engineering reality while writing — not after the spec is "done". | Notion, Confluence, and Google Docs are blank canvases. No writing tool has ever been connected to a live engineering knowledge graph. The PM writing a PRD today has zero organizational context available at authoring time. |
| 7.2 | **Org-Memory-Powered "Similar Feature" Recall** — When a PM starts a new feature, Nudgeio retrieves: the most similar features built in the past, how long they took vs estimate, what caused their rework, which requirements turned out to be technically infeasible, and what the production outcome was. "In 2023 you built a very similar checkout flow. It took 3x the estimated effort because of an undiscovered dependency on the payment service. The engineer who knew about it had left. Here's what the PRD missed." | No tool surfaces past institutional experience at PRD creation time. Engineering teams repeat the same costly mistakes because every PRD starts from zero. |
| 7.3 | **Technical Feasibility Advisor (Real-Time)** — As requirements are written, flags in real-time when a stated requirement: contradicts an existing architectural decision, depends on a service with dangerous knowledge concentration, requires cross-team coordination that has historically caused delays, or assumes a data model that doesn't match how the system actually works. Not a post-review comment — a live signal while the PM is still writing. | No competitor sits at the PRD creation moment. Tech feasibility is evaluated by engineers days or weeks later, after the PM has already committed to stakeholders. |
| 7.4 | **Domain Expert Discovery** — For each section of a PRD, surfaces the engineers with the deepest knowledge of the affected code areas — not by job title, but by knowledge graph contribution depth. "Section 3.2 describes changes to the payment settlement flow. These 2 engineers are the only people with deep context here. Both need to review before this goes to sprint planning." | No tool connects PRD sections to the specific humans with the institutional knowledge needed to validate them. This information exists in the knowledge graph; nobody has surfaced it at the right moment. |
| 7.5 | **Incident History Surfacing** — For any feature touching specific services, automatically surfaces the incident history of those services: frequency, severity, root causes, and which past PRD requirements contributed to those incidents. "The last 3 times a feature touched the notification service, 2 caused P1 incidents within 14 days. Here are the specific edge cases that caused them." | No tool connects PRD authoring to the production incident history of the services being modified. This is the most important risk signal a PM could have — and it's invisible today. |

